{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789235907,"nonce":"CFXKdp4DzF20CMs/R8BXVMUlkNs=","title":"Reactor: the world's source of external wakeups","message":"Not built. Today a world with no ready task blocks in scheduler_idle on its\nendpoint's condvar until a command arrives from another world or the next\ntimer is due (sched/world.rs, native), or naps a millisecond bounded by the\nnext timer on wasm without atomics. Nothing external can wake it: a\nblocking socket read in any language still blocks the OS thread (ash's\nsockets do today), and file watches for hot reload have no home.\n\nThe reactor owns timers, socket readiness, file watches and channels from\nOS threads outside any world. Blocking I/O registers a wait token with it\nand parks; readiness wakes the token through the endpoint, which is the\nseam marked in scheduler_idle. On wasm the host's event source takes the\nsame role and replaces the nap.\n\nDesign in docs/architecture.md, \"Scheduler \u003e The reactor\". Needed by the\nmodule registry's file watches (092f16b) and by any language whose\nblocking calls should park rather than block.","files":null}]}